<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aelia Eudoxia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 404</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aelia Eudoxia (died 404) was the wife of the eastern Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadius">Arcadius</a>, whom she married in 395. The daughter of the Frankish general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauto">Bauto</a>, she was proclaimed augusta in 400 and became a powerful figure at the court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Her conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-chrysostom">John Chrysostom</a> led to his exile; she was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aelia-eudoxia.jpg" alt="Aelia Eudoxia" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aelia_Eudoxia_solidus_(obverse).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eudoxia was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bauto">Flavius Bauto</a>, a Romanised Frank who served as magister militum in the Western Roman army during the 380s. Bauto was last mentioned as consul with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadius">Arcadius</a> in 385 and was dead by 388. Some historians consider her mother to have been Roman, but the primary sources are silent on her maternal ancestry. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zosimus">Zosimus</a>, she began her life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> as a member of the household of Promotus, magister militum of the East, and she is presumed to have been orphaned at the time. She was educated by Pansophius, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-i">Theodosius I</a> died on 17 January 395, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadius">Arcadius</a> succeeded him in the East under the control of Rufinus, the Praetorian prefect, who is reported to have intended to marry his own daughter to the emperor. The eunuch Eutropius instead arranged the marriage with Eudoxia, which took place on 27 April 395 without Rufinus's knowledge. For Eutropius it was an attempt to increase his influence. She was given the title of augusta on 9 January 400, wore the purple paludamentum and appeared on Roman coinage. She and the general Gainas are considered to have played a part in the fall and execution of Eutropius in 399, though how far is disputed.</p><p>Eudoxia became a patron of the Nicene faction of the Church and financed nighttime anti-Arian processions. Her conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-chrysostom">John Chrysostom</a>, Patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, began after he protested over the execution of Eutropius, and she assumed that his denunciations of extravagant dress were aimed at herself. An alliance of Eudoxia, Theophilus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and others held the Synod of the Oak in 403, which deposed and banished John. Arcadius recalled him almost at once, but when a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> statue of her was dedicated near the cathedral John spoke against her harshly, comparing her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodias">Herodias</a>, and he was banished again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eudoxia's children included Pulcheria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a>, Marina and her only son, the future emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a>. Her last pregnancy ended in a miscarriage or, according to the hostile pseudo-Martyrius, a stillbirth, and she died of an infection on 6 October 404. She was buried in the Church of the Holy Apostles in a porphyry sarcophagus. Historians disagree about her influence: Philostorgius thought her more intelligent than her husband, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zosimus">Zosimus</a> thought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuchs</a> manipulated her, and the Cambridge Ancient History reports her dominating the government from 400 to 404.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aelia Eudoxia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late 4th century Franks such as Bauto, Richomer and Arbogast, and probably Merobaudes, became commanders of the Roman army, several of them consuls, and Bauto’s daughter <b>Aelia Eudoxia</b> married the emperor Arcadius.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Byzantine Empire. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=394" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Aelia Eudoxia"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=394">What was here in 394 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
